# Counting with a backward rule
#
# Alice can count to one. A recursive backward rule says she can count to a
# number above one if she can count to the number before it. Asked whether
# she can count to ten, the rule works down 10, 9, ... to the fact 1, and a
# forward rule records the passing test. The example shows recursion with
# arithmetic built-ins (math:greaterThan, math:difference).

@base <https://eyereasoner.github.io/eye/reasoning/bcrule/example4.n3> .
@prefix math: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/math#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/#> .

:alice :canCountTo 1 .

# Backward rule: to count to s, where s is above 1, count to n = s - 1.
{
    ?x :canCountTo ?s .
} <= {
    ?s math:greaterThan 1 .
    (?s 1) math:difference ?n .
    ?x :canCountTo ?n .
} .

# Forward rule: the test passes when Alice can count to ten.
{
    :alice :canCountTo 10
} => {
    :TEST :PASS 1
} .
